'''Jürgen Habermas'''{{Efn|{{IPAc-en|UK|ˈ|h|ɑː|b|ər|m|æ|s}}  {{respell|HAH|bər|mass}},<ref>[https://www.collinsdictionary.com/dictionary/english/habermas "Habermas"]. ''[[Collins English Dictionary]]''.</ref> {{IPAc-en|USalso|-|m|ɑː|s}} {{respell|-mahss}}; {{IPA|de|ˈjʏʁɡn̩ ˈhaːbɐmaːs|lang|De-Jürgen Habermas.ogg}}<ref>Max Mangold and Dudenredaktion: ''Duden Aussprachewörterbuch.'' In: ''Der Duden in zwölf Bänden.'' Volume 6, 6th edition, Dudenverlag, Mannheim/Leipzig/Wien/Zürich 2005 {{ISBN|978-3-411-04066-7}}, "Jürgen" p. 446 and "Habermas" p. 383.</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Krech |first1=Eva-Maria |last2=Stock |first2=Eberhard |last3=Hirschfeld |first3=Ursula |last4=Anders |first4=Lutz Christian |title=Deutsches Aussprachewörterbuch |url=https://archive.org/details/deutschesausspra00krec |url-access=limited |trans-title=German Pronunciation Dictionary |language=de |year=2009 |publisher=[[Walter de Gruyter]] |location=Berlin |isbn=978-3-11-018202-6 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/deutschesausspra00krec/page/n572 561], 629}}</ref>}} (18 June 1929 – 14 March 2026) was a German philosopher and [[social theorist]] in the tradition of [[critical theory]] and [[pragmatism]]. His work addressed [[communicative rationality]] and the [[public sphere]]. He held professorships at [[Heidelberg University]] and [[Goethe University Frankfurt]] and directed the [[Max Planck Institute]] in Starnberg.

Associated with the [[Frankfurt School]], Habermas's work focused on the foundations of [[epistemology]] and [[social theory]], the analysis of [[advanced capitalism]] and democracy, the [[rule of law]] in a critical [[Sociocultural evolution|social-evolutionary context]], and contemporary politics, particularly [[German politics]]. His major works include ''[[The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere]]'' (1962), a social history of the emergence and decline of [[bourgeois]] public discourse, and ''[[The Theory of Communicative Action]]'' (1981), which advanced a theory of rationality grounded in interpersonal linguistic communication rather than in instrumental or strategic reason.

He developed the concept of [[discourse ethics]] and argued that [[the Enlightenment]] remained an "unfinished project" requiring correction rather than abandonment. Habermas's theoretical system is devoted to revealing the possibility of [[reason]], [[emancipation]], and rational-critical communication latent in modern institutions and in the human capacity to deliberate and pursue rational interests. Habermas was known for his work on the phenomenon of [[modernity]],<ref>{{cite book |title=The Cambridge Habermas Lexicon |publisher=Cambridge University Press |page=269 |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/cambridge-habermas-lexicon/modernity-and-modernization/5D1ABB7E56FF0F268328DEB7A991C0BD |chapter=Modernity and Modernization|date=2019 |doi=10.1017/9781316771303.070 |last1=Ferrara |first1=Alessandro |isbn=978-1-316-77130-3 }}</ref> particularly with respect to the discussions of [[Rationalization (sociology)|rationalization]] originally set forth by [[Max Weber]]. He was influenced by [[American pragmatism]], [[Action theory (sociology)|action theory]], and [[poststructuralism]].

As a [[public intellectual]], Habermas intervened prominently in the West German {{lang|de|[[Historikerstreit]]}} of 1986, accusing conservative historians of [[relativizing the Holocaust]], and advocated for deeper [[European political integration]]. In his later work, he engaged with the public role of religion in secular societies, including in a widely discussed 2004 dialogue with Cardinal Joseph Ratzinger (later [[Pope Benedict XVI]]).

== Life and career ==
=== Early life ===
Habermas was born in [[Düsseldorf]], Rhine Province, on 18 June 1929.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The philosopher who rejected a €225,000 prize from the UAE – DW – 05/03/2021 |url=https://www.dw.com/en/j%C3%BCrgen-habermas-who-is-the-philosopher-who-rejected-a-prize-from-the-uae/a-49234310 |access-date=28 March 2024 |website=dw.com |language=en}}</ref> He was born with a [[Cleft lip and cleft palate|cleft palate]] and had corrective surgery twice in childhood.<ref>{{cite SSRN |title=Disabling Democracy: How Disability Reconfigures Deliberative Democratic Norms |first1=Clifford |last1=Simplican |last2=Stacy |date=28 October 2017 |ssrn=1451092}}</ref> Habermas later argued that his speech disability made him think differently about the importance of deep dependence and of communication.<ref>{{cite book |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |date=2008 |title=Between Naturalism and Religion: Philosophical Essays |chapter=First}}</ref> Until his graduation from grammar school, he grew up in a staunchly [[Protestant]] environment in [[Gummersbach]] (near [[Cologne]]), where his grandfather {{ill|Friedrich Habermas|de}} had been the director of the local seminary. His father, {{ill|Ernst Habermas|de}}, who was executive director of the {{ill|Cologne Chamber of Industry and Commerce|de|Industrie- und Handelskammer zu Köln}}, joined the [[Nazi Party]] in 1933 and advised it from 1939.<ref name="IA">{{Cite journal |last=Ahmad |first=Irfan |title=Habermas as an Ethnic Thinker ''par excellence'': On Critique, Palestine and the Role of Intellectuals |journal=[[Teaching in Higher Education]] |volume=30 |issue=6 |year=2025 |page=1346 |doi=10.1080/13562517.2025.2466001}}</ref>

A teenager during [[World War II]], Habermas joined the ''[[Deutsches Jungvolk]]'', a junior section of the [[Hitler Youth]], at his father's instigation,<ref name="IA" /> and rose to the rank of ''Jungvolkführer'' (leader), which allowed him to remain in this formation beyond the age of 14.<ref name="AN">{{Cite journal |last=Neumann |first=Alexander |title=Habermas, philosophe allemand de la restauration française? |journal=[[Variations (journal)|Variations]] |volume=22 |year=2019 |issue=22 |page=2 |url=https://journals.openedition.org/variations/pdf/1061 |doi=10.4000/variations.1061 |access-date=23 November 2025 |archive-date=29 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190429132136/https://journals.openedition.org/variations/pdf/1061 |url-status=dead}}</ref> He organised [[first aid]] training as part of [[medical corps]] service.<ref name="JB">{{cite news |last=Busche |first=Jürgen |title=Hat Habermas die Wahrheit verschluckt? |work=[[Netzeitung]] |date=26 October 2006 |url=http://www.netzeitung.de/voiceofgermany/448936.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061112004547/http://www.netzeitung.de/voiceofgermany/448936.html |archive-date=12 November 2006 |url-status=dead}}</ref> From August 1944, his detachment operated [[Anti-aircraft warfare|anti-aircraft]] guns against the [[Siegfried Line campaign|Allied advances]] on the [[Siegfried Line]].<ref name="WO">{{Cite journal |last=Outhwaite |first=William |title=Review of ''Habermas: A Biography'' by Stefan Müller-Doohm, Cambridge: Polity Press, 2016 |journal=Studies in Social and Political Thought |volume=26 |year=2016 |page=131 |url=http://journals.sussex.ac.uk/index.php/sspt/article/download/65/58 |access-date=23 November 2025 |archive-date=21 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180721204348/http://journals.sussex.ac.uk/index.php/sspt/article/download/65/58 |url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book |last=Specter |first=Matthew G. |title=Habermas: An Intellectual Biography |location=New York |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2010 |page=4 |isbn=978-1-139-49056-6 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gnBy4LB1XiUC&pg=PA4}}</ref> He narrowly avoided being drafted into the [[Wehrmacht]] at a closing stage of the war, shortly before the arrival of American troops near his home.<ref name="WO" />

=== Education and career ===
Habermas studied at the universities of [[University of Göttingen|Göttingen]] (1949–1950), [[University of Zurich|Zurich]] (1950–1951), and [[University of Bonn|Bonn]] (1951–1954) and earned a doctorate in philosophy from Bonn in February 1954 with a dissertation written on the tension between the [[Absolute (philosophy)|absolute]] and history in [[Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling|Schelling]]'s thought, entitled ''{{lang|de|Das Absolute und die Geschichte. Von der Zwiespältigkeit in Schellings Denken}}'' ("The Absolute and History: On the Schism in Schelling's Thought").<ref name="Habermas p. ">{{cite journal |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |title=Das Absolute und die Geschichte |trans-title=The Absolute and History |journal=University Library Heidelberg |year=1954 |doi=10.11588/DIGLIT.41402 |url=https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/habermas1954 |language=de |access-date=3 December 2022 |page= |archive-date=3 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221203161741/https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/habermas1954 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gottfried |first=Paul |date=2026-05-01 |title=Habermas’s Age |url=https://www.theamericanconservative.com/habermass-age/ |access-date=2026-05-08 |website=The American Conservative |language=en-US}}</ref> His dissertation committee included [[Erich Rothacker]] and [[Oskar Becker]],<ref name="Habermas p. " /> both of whom were former Nazis.<ref name="JB" /> Habermas later described the approach of his thesis as [[Heideggerian terminology|Heideggerian]] and noted that it led him to the work of [[young Marx]] via [[Karl Löwith]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Edgar |first=Andrew |title=The Philosophy of Habermas |location=Chesham |publisher=Acumen |year=2005 |page=3 |isbn=1-902683-93-5}}</ref>

In the mid-1950s, Habermas worked briefly as a journalist.{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=127}} His 1953 article for the right-wing daily ''[[Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung]]'' expressed outrage at the publication of [[Martin Heidegger]]'s 1935 lectures (''[[Introduction to Metaphysics (Heidegger book)|Introduction to Metaphysics]]'') that contained a reference to the "inner truth and greatness" of [[Nazism]],<ref>Patrick Bahners: ''Frankfurter Lehre'' In: Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung 2026, March 16, Page 1</ref> while defending a complete separation between Heidegger's philosophy and politics.{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=126}}<ref name="AN" /> Habermas's essay "The Dialectic of Rationalisation" of 1954 sketched the outline for his later work, including his critical engagement with the [[Western Marxists]].{{sfn|Edgar|2005|p=4}}

In 1956, Habermas became [[Theodor W. Adorno]]'s research assistant at the [[University of Frankfurt am Main]]'s [[University of Frankfurt Institute for Social Research|Institute for Social Research]] (IfS).{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=127}}{{sfn|Edgar|2005|p=4}} From 1956 to 1959, he studied philosophy and sociology under Adorno and the fellow [[critical theorist]] [[Max Horkheimer]] at the IfS. He was involved at the time in the early [[Anti-nuclear movement in Germany|anti-nuclear movement]].{{sfn|Neumann|2019|p=9}} His work and activities soon provoked strong objections from Horkheimer, who tried to block the publication of ''Student und Politik. Eine soziologische Untersuchung zum politischen Bewusstsein Frankfurter Studenten'' written by Habermas with [[Ludwig von Friedeburg]] and three others (on the grounds that it would "encourage" the [[Socialist Unity Party of Germany|East German Communists]] and "play into the hands of the potential [[fascists]] at home"), demanded that Adorno sack Habermas as his assistant in 1958, and made unacceptable demands for revision of his [[dissertation]].{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=127}}<ref name=cjc352>{{cite book |first=Craig J. |last=Calhoun |title=Contemporary Sociological Theory |publisher=[[Wiley-Blackwell]] |date=2002 |page=352 |isbn=0-631-21350-3}}</ref> 

Combined with his own belief that the [[Frankfurt School]] had become paralyzed with political skepticism and disdain for [[modern culture]],<ref name=cjc352 /> the conflict resulted in Habermas leaving Frankfurt and finishing his [[habilitation]] in [[political science]] at the [[University of Marburg]] under the Marxist [[Wolfgang Abendroth]]. His 1961 habilitation work was entitled ''{{lang|de|Strukturwandel der Öffentlichkeit. Untersuchungen zu einer Kategorie der bürgerlichen Gesellschaft}}'' (published in English translation in 1989 as ''[[The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere]]: An Inquiry into a Category of Bourgeois Society'').{{Sfn|Specter|2010|pp=33, 226}} It is a detailed social history of the development of the [[bourgeois]] [[public sphere]] from its origins in the 18th-century salons up to its transformation through the influence of capital-driven mass media.{{Sfn|Specter|2010|p=27}}
[[File:AdornoHorkheimerHabermasbyJeremyJShapiro2.png|thumb|Habermas (far right) in Heidelberg, 1964, with Horkheimer (front left) and Adorno (front right)]]

In 1961, Habermas became a ''[[Privatdozent]]'' in Marburg, and—in a move that was highly unusual for the German academic scene of that time—he was offered the position of "extraordinary professor" (professor without chair) of philosophy at the [[University of Heidelberg]] (at the instigation of [[Hans-Georg Gadamer]] and [[Karl Löwith]]) in 1962, which he accepted. In 1964, strongly supported by Adorno, Habermas returned to Frankfurt to take over Horkheimer's chair in philosophy and sociology, and reconciled with Horkheimer, who provided a glowing reference for him to the [[American Jewish Committee]] in 1965.{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=128}} The philosopher [[Albrecht Wellmer]] was Habermas's assistant in Frankfurt from 1966 to 1970.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Susen |first=Simon |chapter=Jürgen Habermas |year=2020 |title=The Cambridge Handbook of Social Theory |volume=1: ''A Contested Canon'' |page=370 |editor-last=Kivisto |editor-first=Peter |place=Cambridge |publisher=Cambridge University Press |doi=10.1017/9781316677445.019 |isbn=978-1-107-16264-8}}</ref>

Following Adorno's death in 1969, Habermas, who had earlier declined the directorship of the Institute for Social Research, recommended [[Leszek Kołakowski]] to take up the role in the following year. When the proposal fell through due to opposition from the philosophy department, Habermas published an open letter against the institutionalisation of critical theory.{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=129}} 

In 1971, he accepted the position of co-director (alongside [[Carl Friedrich von Weizsäcker]]{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=132}}) of the {{ill|Max Planck Institute for the Study of the Scientific and Technical World|de|Max-Planck-Institut zur Erforschung der Lebensbedingungen der wissenschaftlich-technischen Welt}} in [[Starnberg]], near [[Munich]], and worked there until 1983, two years after the publication of his [[Masterpiece|magnum opus]], ''[[The Theory of Communicative Action]]''. He proclaimed his definitive break with the Frankfurt School of critical theory in a 1971 letter to [[Herbert Marcuse]].{{sfn|Neumann|2019|p=5}} Habermas served as the [[doctoral advisor]] to [[Hans-Hermann Hoppe]], who earned his doctorate in 1974,<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |last=Dieterle |first=David A. |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Economic_Thinkers/mhfHEAAAQBAJ |encyclopedia=Economic Thinkers: A Biographical Encyclopedia |date=2013-08-08 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing USA |isbn=978-0-313-39747-9 |language=en |title=Hoppe, Hans-Hermann |p=145}}</ref> although he came to reject much of Habermas's thought.<ref>[[Lew Rockwell]], introduction to Hoppe's ''A Short History of Man'' (2015), Auburn, Mississippi: Mises Institute, p. 9.</ref> He was elected a Foreign Honorary Member of the [[American Academy of Arts and Sciences]] in 1984.<ref name=AAAS>{{cite web |title=Book of Members, 1780–2010: Chapter H |url=https://www.amacad.org/sites/default/files/academy/multimedia/pdfs/publications/bookofmembers/ChapterH.pdf|publisher=American Academy of Arts and Sciences |access-date=31 December 2024 |archive-url= |archive-date=}}</ref>

[[File:Jürgen Habermas (1986).png|thumb|upright|Habermas in 1986]]

In 1983, Habermas returned to his chair at Frankfurt. In 1986, he received the [[Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz Prize]] of the {{lang|de|[[Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft]]}}, which is the highest honour awarded in German research. Since he retired from Frankfurt in 1994, Habermas continued to publish extensively. He held the position of "permanent visiting" professor at [[Northwestern University]] in Evanston, Illinois, and "[[Theodor Heuss]] Professor" at [[The New School]], New York City.{{Sfn|Susen|2020|pp=370–371}} He was elected a foreign member of the [[Serbian Academy of Sciences and Arts]] on 15 December 1988.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Habermas Jirgen |url=https://www.sanu.ac.rs/clan/habermas-jirgen/ |access-date=2026-03-14 |website=www.sanu.ac.rs |archive-date=17 November 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117002118/https://www.sanu.ac.rs/clan/habermas-jirgen/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Habermas was awarded the [[Prince of Asturias Award]] in Social Sciences of 2003.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 May 2003 |title=Jürgen Habermas |url=https://www.fpa.es/en/princess-of-asturias-awards/laureates/2003-jurgen-habermas/ |access-date=14 March 2026 |website=Fundación Princesa de Asturias |language=en}}</ref> Habermas was also the 2004 Kyoto Laureate<ref>Jürgen Habermas (2004) [http://homepage.mac.com/gedavis/JH/Kyoto_lecture_Nov_2004.pdf Public space and political public sphere – the biographical roots of two motifs in my thought.](Commemorative Lecture, Kyoto) {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120204151440/http://homepage.mac.com/gedavis/JH/Kyoto_lecture_Nov_2004.pdf|date=4 February 2012}} (pp. 2–4)</ref> in the [[Kyoto Prize in Arts and Philosophy|Arts and Philosophy]] section. He traveled to [[San Diego]] and on 5 March 2005, as part of the [[University of San Diego]]'s Kyoto Symposium, gave a speech entitled ''The Public Role of Religion in Secular Context'', regarding the evolution of [[separation of church and state]] from neutrality to intense [[secularism]]. He received the 2005 [[Holberg International Memorial Prize]] (about €520,000). In 2007, Habermas was listed as the seventh most-cited author in the [[humanities]] (including the [[social science]]s) by ''[[The Times Higher Education Guide]]'', ahead of Max Weber and behind [[Erving Goffman]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.timeshighereducation.co.uk/story.asp?storyCode=405956&sectioncode=26 |title=The most cited authors of books in the humanities |publisher=timeshighereducation.co.uk |date=26 March 2009 |access-date=16 November 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110423053928/http://www.timeshighereducation.co.uk/story.asp?storyCode=405956&sectioncode=26 |archive-date=23 April 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Bibliometrics|Bibliometric]] studies demonstrate his continuing influence and increasing relevance.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Buhmann |first1=Alexander |last2=Ihlen |first2=Øyvind |last3=Aaen-Stockdale |first3=Craig |date=4 November 2019 |title=Connecting the dots: a bibliometric review of Habermasian theory in public relations research |url=https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JCOM-12-2018-0127/full/html |journal=[[Journal of Communication Management]] |language=en |volume=23 |issue=4 |pages=444–467 |doi=10.1108/JCOM-12-2018-0127 |hdl=10852/74987 |s2cid=210568613 |issn=1363-254X |hdl-access=free |archive-date=20 August 2020 |access-date=3 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200820031438/https://www.emerald.com/insight/content/doi/10.1108/JCOM-12-2018-0127/full/html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Scholars taught or influenced by Habermas included [[Jóhann P. Árnason]], [[Axel Honneth]], [[Hans Joas]], [[Claus Offe]], and {{ill|Herbert Schnädelbach|de}}.{{Sfn|Susen|2020|p=371}}

===Personal life and death===
Habermas married Ute Wesselhoeft (1930–2025),<ref>{{Cite news |last=von Thadden |first=Elisabeth |date=17 June 2025 |title=Ute Habermas-Wesselhoeft: Die Ermöglicherin |url=https://www.zeit.de/2025/26/ute-habermas-wesselhoeft-juergen-ehe-tod-philosophie |access-date=14 March 2026 |work=[[Die Zeit]] |language=de |archive-date=9 February 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260209154640/https://www.zeit.de/2025/26/ute-habermas-wesselhoeft-juergen-ehe-tod-philosophie |url-status=live }}</ref> a teacher, in 1955. They had three children together.<ref>{{Cite encyclopedia |last=Greenberg |first=Nina Manasan |last2=Martin |first2=Michael S.  |url=http://archive.org/details/twentiethcentury0000unse_k1p9 |title=Jürgen Habermas |encyclopedia=[[Dictionary of Literary Biography]] |volume=242 |page=190 |year=2001 |publisher=Gale Group |isbn=978-0-7876-4659-2}}</ref> One of their children, [[Rebekka Habermas|Rebekka]] (1959–2023), later became professor of modern history at the [[University of Göttingen]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Beckerman |first=Gal |date=2026-03-14 |title=Jürgen Habermas Dies at 96; One of Postwar Germany's Most Influential Thinkers |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/14/books/jurgen-habermas-dead.html |access-date=2026-03-14 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Habermas died in [[Starnberg]], where he had lived since 1994, on 14 March 2026, at the age of 96.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Moulson |first=Gier |date=14 March 2026 |title=Jürgen Habermas, influential German philosopher, dies at 96 |url=https://apnews.com/article/juergen-habermas-dead-germany-2b541721af6cb19abfaa923359d091b5 |access-date=14 March 2026 |work=[[AP News]]}}</ref><ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20220713043537/http://agso.uni-graz.at/lexikon/klassiker/habermas/21bio.htm "Biografie Jürgen Habermas"]. ''agso.uni-graz.at'' (in German). Retrieved 14 March 2026.</ref> According to ''[[De Standaard]]'', he remained "intellectually alert" even in his final years.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Reynebeau |first=Marc |date=15 March 2026 |title=Jürgen Habermas, van 'Hitlerknaap' tot voorvechter van de democratie |url=https://www.standaard.be/buitenland/jurgen-habermas-van-hitlerknaap-tot-voorvechter-van-de-democratie/142113762.html |access-date=16 March 2026 |work=[[De Standaard]] |language=nl}}</ref>

== Philosophy and social theory ==
{{Frankfurt School sidebar}}
Habermas constructed a comprehensive framework of philosophy and social theory drawing on a number of intellectual traditions:<ref name="Habermas, Jurgen 1981">{{cite book |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |date=1981 |title=Kleine Politische Schrifen I-IV |language=de |page=500}}</ref>
* the [[German philosophy|German philosophical thought]] of [[Immanuel Kant]], [[Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling|Friedrich Schelling]], [[Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel|G. W. F. Hegel]], [[Wilhelm Dilthey]], [[Edmund Husserl]] and [[Hans-Georg Gadamer]]
* the [[Marxist philosophy|Marxian]] tradition—both the theory of [[Karl Marx]] as well as the critical [[Neo-Marxism|neo-Marxian]] theory of the [[Frankfurt School]], i.e. [[Max Horkheimer]], [[Theodor W. Adorno]] and [[Herbert Marcuse]].<ref name="Habermas, Jurgen 1981"/>
* the sociological theories of [[Max Weber]], [[Émile Durkheim]] and [[George Herbert Mead]]
* the [[linguistic philosophy]] and [[speech act]] theories of [[Ludwig Wittgenstein]], [[J. L. Austin]], [[P. F. Strawson]], [[Stephen Toulmin]] and [[John Searle]]
* the developmental psychology of [[Jean Piaget]] and [[Lawrence Kohlberg]]
* the American pragmatist tradition of [[Charles Sanders Peirce]] and [[John Dewey]]
* the sociological [[social system|social systems theory]] of [[Talcott Parsons]] and [[Niklas Luhmann]]
* [[Neo-Kantian]] thought<ref>{{cite book |last=Müller-Doohm |first=Stefan |title=Jürgen Habermas |location=Frankfurt |publisher=Suhrkamp |date=2008 |series=Suhrkamp BasisBiographie |volume=38}}</ref>

Jürgen Habermas considered his major contribution to be the development of the concept and theory of communicative reason or communicative rationality,<ref>{{Cite book |last=Lessnoff |first=Michael |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=50lmEm7X9OEC |title=Political Philosophers of the Twentieth Century |date=1999-01-05 |publisher=Wiley |isbn=978-0-631-20261-5 |page=277 |language=en}}</ref> which distinguishes itself from the [[rationalism|rationalist tradition]] by locating [[rationality]] in structures of interpersonal linguistic communication.<ref>{{Cite thesis |last=Beaumaris |first=Arini |title=Moral judgement to moral action - implications for education |year=2010 |degree=PhD |publisher=[[University of Newcastle (Australia)|University of Newcastle]] |url=https://hdl.handle.net/1959.13/805543 |page=31}}</ref> This social theory advances the goals of [[human emancipation]], while maintaining an inclusive [[universalist]] [[morality|moral]] framework.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Chen |first=Xunwu |url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-030-79794-2 |title=The Essentials of Habermas |year=2021 |publisher=Springer International Publishing |isbn=978-3-030-79793-5 |location=Cham |language=en |doi=10.1007/978-3-030-79794-2 |page=61}}</ref> 

This framework rests on the argument called [[universal pragmatics]]—that all speech acts have an inherent [[telos]]—the goal of mutual [[understanding]], and that human beings possess the communicative competence to bring about such understanding.{{Sfn|Chen|2021|pp=36, 40}} Habermas built the framework out of the [[speech-act]] philosophy of [[J. L. Austin]] and [[John Searle]], [[transformational grammar]] of [[Noam Chomsky]], [[Formal semantics (natural language)|formal semantics]] of [[Gottlob Frege]] and [[Michael Dummett]], [[Intension|intentionalist]] semantics of [[Paul Grice]], [[use theory of meaning]] of [[Ludwig Wittgenstein]], [[Hermeneutics#Modern hermeneutics|hermeneutics]] of [[Wilhelm Dilthey]] and [[Hans-Georg Gadamer]], [[transcendental pragmatics]] of [[Karl-Otto Apel]] and [[Inferential role semantics|inferential semantics]] of [[Robert Brandom]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Fultner |first=Barbara |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Jurgen_Habermas/fZ_CBQAAQBAJ |title=Jurgen Habermas: Key Concepts |date=2014-12-05 |publisher=Routledge |isbn=978-1-317-49202-3 |language=en |page=55}}</ref>

Habermas's works resonate within the traditions of Kant and [[the Enlightenment]] and of [[democratic socialism]] through his emphasis on the potential for transforming the world and arriving at a more humane, just, and egalitarian society through the realization of the human potential for reason, in part through discourse ethics. While Habermas has stated that the Enlightenment is an "unfinished project", he argues it should be corrected and complemented, not discarded.<ref name=cjc351>{{harvnb|Calhoun|2002|p=351}}</ref> In this he distances himself from the Frankfurt School, criticizing it, as well as much of [[postmodernist]] thought, for excessive pessimism, [[Political radicalism|radicalism]], and exaggerations.<ref name=cjc351/> Due to Habermas's break with critical theory's original negative assessment of capitalism, [[Gordon Finlayson]] considers him a member of the first-generation Starnberg School rather than second-generation Frankfurt School.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Finlayson |first=James Gordon |author-link=Gordon Finlayson |title=On Kantians and Pragmatists: Kenneth Baynes's Habermas |journal=[[European Journal of Philosophy]] |volume=25 |issue=3 |year=2017 |page=876 |doi=10.1111/ejop.12290}}</ref>{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=132}}

Within sociology, Habermas's major contribution was the development of a comprehensive theory of [[societal evolution]] and modernization focusing on the difference between communicative rationality and rationalization on one hand and strategic/[[instrumental rationality]] and rationalization on the other. This includes a critique from a communicative standpoint of the differentiation-based [[theory]] of [[social system]]s developed by [[Niklas Luhmann]].{{sfn|Outhwaite|2016|p=128}}

Habermas perceives the rationalization, [[humanism|humanization]] and [[democratization]] of society in terms of the [[institutionalization]] of the potential for rationality that is inherent in the [[communicative competence]]<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ray |first=Larry |date=July 2023 |title=[Review of ''Narratives of Disenchantment and Secularization: Critiquing Max Weber's Idea of Modernity ed. by Robert A Yelle and Lorenz Trein''] |journal=Max Weber Studies |volume=23 |issue=2 |page=242 |doi=10.1353/max.2023.a906835 |issn=2056-4074 |s2cid=261930904 |id={{Project MUSE|906835}}}}</ref> that is unique to the [[human species]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Bastalich |first=Wendy |date=March 2009 |title=Reading Foucault: Genealogy and Social Science Research Methodology and Ethics |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.5153/sro.1905 |journal=Sociological Research Online |language=en |volume=14 |issue=2 |page=85 |doi=10.5153/sro.1905 |issn=1360-7804 |archive-date=29 November 2023 |access-date=16 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231129123029/https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.5153/sro.1905 |url-status=live }}</ref> Habermas contends that communicative competence has developed through the course of evolution,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ware |first=Robert X. |date=September 1982 |title=Habermas's Evolutions |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/product/identifier/S0045509100009991/type/journal_article |journal=Canadian Journal of Philosophy |language=en |volume=12 |issue=3 |page=615 |doi=10.1080/00455091.1982.10716350 |issn=0045-5091}}</ref> but in contemporary society it is often suppressed or weakened by the way in which major domains of social life, such as the [[Market (economics)|market]], the [[State (polity)|state]], and organizations, have been given over to or taken over by strategic/instrumental rationality, so that the logic of the system supplants that of the [[lifeworld]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Regmi |first=Kapil Dev |date=2017-11-02 |title=Habermas, lifeworld and rationality: towards a comprehensive model of lifelong learning |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02601370.2017.1377776 |journal=International Journal of Lifelong Education |language=en |volume=36 |issue=6 |page=7 |doi=10.1080/02601370.2017.1377776 |issn=0260-1370 |archive-date=15 December 2024 |access-date=16 March 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241215004702/https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/02601370.2017.1377776 |url-status=live }}</ref>

In his 1981 book ''[[The Theory of Communicative Action]]'', Habermas stated that the "market is the most important example of a norm-free regulation of cooperative contexts".<ref name="IA" /> Habermas had previously distanced himself from the [[Hegelian Marxism]] of [[György Lukács]], Adorno and [[Karl Korsch]] in his lectures delivered from 1973 onwards and published as {{lang|de|Zur Rekonstruktion des Historischen Materialismus}} (French translation as {{lang|fr|Après Marx}}) in 1976.{{sfn|Neumann|2019|pp=7, 15, 18}} In a 1979 interview at Starnberg, after crediting [[Karl-Otto Apel]] with first labelling him a [[neo-Marxist]] around the time of his habilitation in 1961, Habermas commented: "Today I value being considered a Marxist". He added that he was "not a Marxist in the sense of believing in Marxism as a sure-fire explanation. Still, Marxism did give me both the impetus and the analytical means to investigate the development of the relationship between democracy and capitalism".<ref>{{cite interview |interviewer-last1=Horster |interviewer-first1=Detlef |interviewer-last2=van Reijen |interviewer-first2=Willem |title=Interview with Jürgen Habermas Starnberg, March 23, 1979 |journal=[[New German Critique]] |volume=18 |year=1979 |pages=33–34 |jstor=487849}}</ref> He claimed to be "the last Marxist" as late as 1989.<ref name="IA" /> [[Harry F. Dahms]] sees Habermas as "one of [the] latest, though not unambivalent, representatives" of [[Neo-Marxism|Weberian Marxism]],<ref>{{cite journal |last=Dahms |first=Harry F. |author-link=Harry Dahms |title=Theory in Weberian Marxism: Patterns of Critical Social Theory in Lukács and Habermas |journal=[[Sociological Theory (journal)|Sociological Theory]] |volume=15 |issue=3 |year=1997 |page=182 |doi=10.1111/0735-2751.00032}}</ref> with Irfan Ahmad arguing that the influence of Max Weber on Habermas (via Talcott Parsons) overrides that of Karl Marx.{{sfn|Ahmad|2025|pp=1347–1348}} Habermas's reading of Marx was criticised as "at best, questionable and, at worst, deeply flawed".{{sfn|Susen|2020|p=388}}

=== Reconstructive science ===
{{main|Rational reconstruction}}
Habermas's concept of "[[Rational reconstruction|reconstructive science]]" – in Italian scholar Luca Corchia's words – "placed the "general theory of society" between philosophy and social science and re-established the rift between the "great theorization" and the "[[empirical research]]"".{{clarify|date=March 2026}} Habermas used rational reconstruction as a model for analysing the development of [[social system]]s in terms of a dialectic between [[Symbolic linguistic representation|symbolic representations]] and [[Reproduction (economics)|material reproduction]]. His survey of social evolution distinguished [[Primitive society|primitive]], [[Traditional society|traditional]], [[Modern society|modern]] and contemporary formations.<ref name="Corchia">{{cite book |first=Luca |last=Corchia |chapter=Explicative models of complexity. The reconstructions of social evolution for Jürgen Habermas |editor1-first=S |editor-last=Balbi |editor2-first=G |editor2-last=Scepi |editor3-first=G |editor3-last=Russolillo |editor4-first=A |display-editors=3 |editor4-last=Stawinoga |chapter-url=http://www.jovene.it/articoli/details.php?nome=Catalogo&ID=38140&Tipo=listino&Oggetto=Novit%C3%A0 |title=Book of Short Abstracts |volume=7th International Conference on Social Science Methodology&nbsp;– RC33&nbsp;– Logic and Methodology in Sociology |place=Napoli, IT |date=1 September 2008 |publisher=Jovene Editore |archive-date=11 December 2008 |access-date=25 October 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081211073524/http://www.jovene.it/articoli/details.php?nome=Catalogo&ID=38140&Tipo=listino&Oggetto=Novit%C3%A0 |url-status=dead |quote=Habermas introduces the concept of 'reconstructive science' with a double purpose: to place the 'general theory of society' between philosophy and social science and re-establish the rift between the 'great theorization' and the 'empirical research'. The model of '[[rational reconstruction]]s' represents the main thread of the surveys about the 'structures' of the world of life ('culture', 'society' and 'personality') and their respective 'functions' (cultural reproductions, social integrations and socialization). For this purpose, the dialectics between 'symbolic representation' of 'the structures subordinated to all worlds of life' ('internal relationships') and the 'material reproduction' of the social systems in their complex ('external relationships' between social systems and environment) has to be considered. This model finds an application, above all, in the 'theory of the social evolution', starting from the reconstruction of the necessary conditions for a [[Phylogenetic tree|phylogeny]] of the socio-cultural life forms (the 'hominization') until an analysis of the development of 'social formations', which Habermas subdivides into primitive, traditional, modern and contemporary formations. This paper is an attempt, primarily, to formalize the model of 'reconstruction of the logic of development' of 'social formations' summed up by Habermas through the differentiation between vital world and social systems (and, within them, through the 'rationalization of the world of life' and the 'growth in complexity of the social systems'). Secondly, it tries to offer some methodological clarifications about the 'explanation of the dynamics' of 'historical processes' and, in particular, about the 'theoretical meaning' of the evolutional theory's propositions. Even if the German sociologist considers that the 'ex-post rational reconstructions' and 'the models system/environment' cannot have a complete 'historiographical application', these certainly act as a general premise in the argumentative structure of the 'historical explanation'.}}</ref>

=== Public sphere ===
{{Further|Public sphere}}

In ''[[The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere]]'', Habermas argues that prior to the 18th century, European culture had been dominated by a "representational" culture, where one party sought to "represent" itself on its audience by overwhelming its subjects.<ref name=Blanning26>{{cite book |last=Blanning |first=T. C. W. |title=The French Revolution Class War or Culture Clash? |location=New York |publisher=St. Martin's Press |orig-date=1987 |edition=2nd |date=1998 |page=26}}</ref> As an example of "representational" culture, Habermas argued that [[Louis XIV]]'s [[Palace of Versailles]] was meant to show the greatness of the French state and its King by overpowering the senses of visitors to the Palace.<ref name=Blanning26 /> Habermas identifies "representational" culture as corresponding to the feudal stage of development according to Marxist theory, arguing that the coming of the capitalist stage of development marked the appearance of {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} (the public sphere).<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. pages 26-27">{{harvnb|Blanning|1998|pp=26–27}}</ref> In the culture characterized by {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}}, there occurred a public space outside of the control by the state, where individuals exchanged views and knowledge.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27">{{harvnb|Blanning|1998|p=27}}</ref>

In Habermas's view, the growth in newspapers, [[Literary magazine|journals]], reading clubs, [[Masonic lodge]]s, and [[coffeehouse]]s in 18th-century Europe, all in different ways, marked the gradual replacement of "representational" culture with {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} culture.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27" /> Habermas argued that the essential characteristic of the {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} culture was its "critical" nature.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27" /> Unlike "representational" culture where only one party was active and the other passive, the {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} culture was characterized by a dialogue as individuals either met in conversation, or exchanged views via the print media.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27" /> 

Habermas maintains that as Britain was the most liberal country in Europe, the culture of the public sphere emerged there first around 1700, and the growth of {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} culture took place over most of the 18th century in Continental Europe.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27" /> In his view, the [[French Revolution]] was in large part caused by the collapse of "representational" culture, and its replacement by {{lang|de|Öffentlichkeit}} culture.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. page 27" /> Though Habermas's main concern in ''The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere'' was to expose what he regarded as the deceptive nature of free institutions in the West, his book had a major effect on the historiography of the French Revolution.<ref name="Blanning, T.C.W. pages 26-27" />

According to Habermas, a variety of factors resulted in the eventual decay of the public sphere, including the growth of a commercial mass media, which turned the critical public into a passive consumer public;<ref>{{Cite book |chapter=Introduction |page=11 |last=Kegley |first=Jacquelyn |editor-last=Kegley |editor-first=Jacquelyn |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Persuasion_and_Compulsion_in_Democracy/C-sd9BkOdbAC |title=Persuasion and Compulsion in Democracy |editor2-last=Skowronski |editor2-first=Krzysztof Piotr |date=2013-01-25 |publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing PLC |isbn=978-0-7391-7879-9 |language=en}}</ref> and the welfare state, which merged the state with society so thoroughly that the public sphere was squeezed out. It also turned the "public sphere" into a site of self-interested contestation for the resources of the state rather than a space for the development of a public-minded [[rational consensus]].<ref>{{Cite book |last=Kellner |first=Douglas |chapter=Habermas, the Public Sphere, and Democracy |date=2014 |title=Re-Imagining Public Space: The Frankfurt School in the 21st Century |pages=22–23 |editor-last=Boros |editor-first=Diana |editor2-last=Glass |editor2-first=James M. |chapter-url=https://doi.org/10.1057/9781137373311_2 |access-date=2026-03-16 |location=New York |publisher=Palgrave Macmillan US |doi=10.1057/9781137373311_2 |isbn=978-1-137-37331-1 |language=en}}</ref>

His most known work to date, ''[[The Theory of Communicative Action]]'' (1981), is based on an adaptation of Talcott Parsons' [[AGIL paradigm]]. In this work, Habermas voiced criticism of the process of modernization, which he saw as inflexible direction forced through by economic and administrative rationalization.<ref name=cjc353>{{harvnb|Calhoun|2002|p=353}}</ref> Habermas outlined how our everyday lives are penetrated by formal systems as parallel to development of the [[welfare state]], [[corporate capitalism]] and [[mass consumption]].<ref name=cjc353/> These reinforcing trends rationalize public life.<ref name=cjc353/> Disfranchisement of citizens occurs as political parties and interest groups become rationalized and [[representative democracy]] replaces [[participatory democracy]].<ref name=cjc353/> 

In consequence, boundaries between public and private, the individual and society, the [[Social system|system]] and the lifeworld are deteriorating.<ref name=cjc353/> Democratic public life cannot develop where matters of public importance are not discussed by citizens.<ref name=cjc354>{{harvnb|Calhoun|2002|p=354}}</ref> An "[[ideal speech situation]]"<ref>{{cite book |title=Democracy, Power and Legitimacy: The Critical Theory of Jürgen Habermas |last=Payrow Shabani |first=Omid A. |year=2003 |publisher=University of Toronto Press |isbn= 978-0-8020-8761-4 |page=49 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=tKDU3l3cq60C&pg=PA49}}</ref> requires participants to have the same capacities of discourse, social equality and their words are not confused by ideology or other errors.<ref name=cjc354/> In this version of the [[consensus theory of truth]] Habermas maintains that truth is what would be agreed upon in an ideal speech situation.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ferrara |first=Alessandro |date=January 1987 |title=a critique of habermas's consensus theory of truth |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/019145378701300104 |journal=Philosophy & Social Criticism |language=en |volume=13 |issue=1 |page=44 |doi=10.1177/019145378701300104 |issn=0191-4537}}</ref>

Habermas has expressed optimism about the possibility of the revival of the public sphere.<ref name=cjc355>{{harvnb|Calhoun|2002|p=355}}</ref> He discerns a hope for the future where the representative democracy-reliant [[nation-state]] is replaced by a [[deliberative democracy]]-reliant political organism based on the equal rights and obligations of citizens.<ref name=cjc355/> In such a direct democracy-driven system, the activist public sphere is needed for debates on matters of public importance as well as the mechanism for that discussion to affect the [[decision-making process]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Carty |first=Victoria |date=2010 |title=New Information Communication Technologies and Grassroots Mobilization |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13691180902915658 |journal=Information, Communication & Society |language=en |volume=13 |issue=2 |page=159 |doi=10.1080/13691180902915658 |issn=1369-118X}}</ref>

== Key dialogues and engagement with politics ==
[[File:Habermas29 (14307317234).jpg|thumb|Habermas demonstrating his [[European Union citizenship]] with his passport during a 2014 lecture]]

=== Positivism dispute ===
{{Main|Positivism dispute}}

The [[positivism dispute]] was a political-philosophical dispute between the critical rationalists ([[Karl Popper]], [[Hans Albert]]) and the Frankfurt School ([[Theodor Adorno]], Habermas) in 1961, about the methodology of the social sciences. It grew into a broad discussion within German sociology from 1961 to 1969.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Heller |first=Agnes |last2=Ritter |first2=Mark |date=1978 |title=The Positivism Dispute as a Turning Point in German Post-War Theory |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/487905 |journal=New German Critique |issue=15 |pages=49–56 |doi=10.2307/487905 |issn=0094-033X}}</ref>

===Habermas and Gadamer===
There is a controversy between Habermas and [[Hans-Georg Gadamer]] about limits of [[hermeneutics]]. Gadamer completed his magnum opus, ''Truth and Method'', in 1960, and engaged in his debate with Habermas over the possibility of transcending history and culture to find a truly objective position from which to critique society.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Brenkman |first=John |url=https://books.google.com/books?hl=en&lr=&id=Ru2YDwAAQBAJ |title=Culture and Domination |date=2019-05-15 |publisher=Cornell University Press |isbn=978-1-5017-4554-6 |page=26 |language=en}}</ref>

During the 1960s, Gadamer supported Habermas and advocated for him to be offered a job at Heidelberg before he had completed his habilitation, despite Max Horkheimer's objections. While they both criticized positivism, a philosophical disagreement arose between them in the 1970s. This disagreement expanded the scope of Gadamer's philosophical influence. Despite fundamental agreements between them, such as starting from the hermeneutic tradition and returning to Greek practical philosophy, Habermas argued that Gadamer's emphasis on tradition and prejudice blinded him to the ideological operation of power. Habermas believed that Gadamer's approach failed to enable critical reflection on the sources of ideology in society. He accused Gadamer of endorsing a dogmatic stance toward tradition, which made it difficult to identify distortions in understanding. Gadamer countered that refusing the universal nature of hermeneutics was the more dogmatic stance because it affirmed the deception that the subject can free itself from the past.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Barthold |first1=Lauren Swayne |title=Hans-Georg Gadamer (1900—2002) |url=https://iep.utm.edu/gadamer/ |website=Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy |access-date=22 February 2023 |archive-date=7 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230307022617/https://iep.utm.edu/gadamer/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Habermas and Foucault ===
{{Main|Foucault–Habermas debate}}

There is a dispute concerning whether [[Michel Foucault]]'s ideas of "power analytics" and "[[Genealogy (philosophy)|genealogy]]" or Jürgen Habermas's ideas of "[[communicative rationality]]" and "[[discourse ethics]]" provides a better critique of the nature of power in society. The debate compares and evaluates the central ideas of Habermas and Foucault as they pertain to questions of [[Power (sociology)|power]], [[reason]], ethics, [[modernity]], democracy, [[civil society]], and [[social action]].<ref>{{Cite conference |last=Flyvbjerg |first=Bent |author-link=Bent Flyvbjerg |date=1 April 2000 |title=Ideal Theory, Real Rationality: Habermas Versus Foucault and Nietzsche |url=https://ssrn.com/abstract=2278421 |conference=Paper for the Political Studies Association's 50th Annual Conference, The Challenges for Democracy in the 21st Century, London School of Economics and Political Science |pages=1-20 |doi=10.2139/ssrn.2278421 |ssrn=2278421}}</ref>

=== Habermas and Apel ===
Habermas and [[Karl-Otto Apel]] both support a postmetaphysical, universal moral theory, but they disagree on the nature and justification of this principle. Apel asserts that the principle is a transcendental condition of human activity. Habermas disagrees, arguing that Apel is too concerned with transcendental conditions. Meanwhile, Apel argues that Habermas does not value critical discourse sufficiently.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Gamwell |first=Franklin I. |title=Habermas and Apel on communicative ethics: Their difference and the difference it makes |journal=[[Philosophy and Social Criticism]] |date=1997 |volume=23 |issue=2 |pages=21–45 |doi=10.1177/019145379702300202 |s2cid=144776550 |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/019145379702300202 |archive-date=7 April 2023 |access-date=22 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230407181631/https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/019145379702300202 |url-status=live }}</ref>

=== Habermas and Rawls ===
{{Main|Habermas–Rawls debate}}

There is a debate between Habermas and [[John Rawls]]. The debate centers around the question of how to do political philosophy under conditions of cultural pluralism, if the aim of political philosophy is to uncover the normative foundation of a modern [[liberal democracy]]. Habermas believes that Rawls's view is inconsistent with the idea of popular sovereignty, while Rawls argues that political legitimacy is solely a matter of sound moral reasoning or that democratic will formation has been unduly downgraded in his theory.<ref>{{cite journal |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |date=1995 |title=Reconciliation Through the Public Use of Reason: Remarks on John Rawls's Political Liberalism |journal=[[Journal of Philosophy]] |volume=92 |number=3 |pages=109–131}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last=Rawls |first=John |date=1995 |title=Political Liberalism: Reply to Habermas |journal=[[Journal of Philosophy]] |volume=92 |number=3 |pages=132–180}}</ref>

===''Historikerstreit'' (historians' dispute)===
{{Main|Historikerstreit}}

Habermas is famous both as a [[public intellectual]] and as a scholar; most notably, in the 1980s he used the [[popular press]] to attack the German historians [[Ernst Nolte]], [[Michael Stürmer]], [[Klaus Hildebrand]] and [[Andreas Hillgruber]]. Habermas first expressed his views on these historians in ''[[Die Zeit]]'' on 11 July 1986 in a ''[[feuilleton]]'' (a type of culture and arts opinion essay in German newspapers) entitled "A Kind of Settlement of Damages". Habermas criticized Nolte, Hildebrand, Stürmer and Hillgruber for "apologistic" history writing in regard to the Nazi era, and for seeking to "close Germany's opening to the West" that in Habermas's view had existed since 1945.<ref name="Piper43">{{cite book |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |chapter=A Kind of Settlement of Damages On Apologetic Tendencies in German History Writing |pages=34–44 [43] |title=Forever In the Shadow of Hitler? |editor-first=Ernst |editor-last=Piper |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref>

Habermas argued that Nolte, Stürmer, Hildebrand and Hillgruber had tried to detach Nazi rule and the [[The Holocaust|Holocaust]] from the mainstream of [[German history]], explain away Nazism as a reaction to [[Bolshevism]], and partially rehabilitate the [[reputation of the Wehrmacht]] (German Army) during [[World War II]]. Habermas wrote that Stürmer was trying to create a "vicarious religion" in German history which, together with the work of Hillgruber, glorifying the last days of the German Army on the Eastern Front, was intended to serve as a "kind of NATO philosophy colored with German nationalism".<ref>{{cite book |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |chapter=A Kind of Settlement of Damages On Apologetic Tendencies in German History Writing |pages=34–44 [42–43] |title=Forever In the Shadow of Hitler? |editor-first=Ernst |editor-last=Piper |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref> About Hillgruber's statement that [[Adolf Hitler]] wanted to exterminate the Jews "because only such a 'racial revolution' could lend permanence to the world-power status of his ''Reich''", Habermas wrote: "Since Hillgruber does not use the verb in the subjunctive, one does not know whether the historian has adopted the perspective of the particulars this time too".<ref>{{cite book |last=Habermas |first=Jürgen |chapter=A Kind of Settlement of Damages On Apologetic Tendencies in German History Writing |pages=34–44 [37–38] |title=Forever In the Shadow of Hitler? |editor-first=Ernst |editor-last=Piper |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref>

Habermas wrote: "The unconditional opening of the Federal Republic to the political culture of the West is the greatest intellectual achievement of our postwar period; my generation should be especially proud of this. This event cannot and should not be stabilized by a kind of NATO philosophy colored with German nationalism. The opening of the Federal Republic has been achieved precisely by overcoming the ideology of Central Europe that our revisionists are trying to warm up for us with their geopolitical drumbeat about "the old geographically central position of the Germans in Europe" (Stürmer) and "the reconstruction of the destroyed European Center" (Hillgruber). The only patriotism that will not estrange us from the West is a [[constitutional patriotism]]."<ref name="Piper43"/>

The debate known as the ''Historikerstreit'' ("Historians' Dispute") was not at all one-sided, because Habermas was himself attacked by scholars like [[Joachim Fest]],<ref>{{cite book |last=Fest |first=Joachim |author-link=Joachim Fest |chapter=Encumbered Remembrance: The Controversy about the Incomparability of National-Socialist Mass Crimes |pages=63–71 [64–65] |title=Forever In The Shadow of Hitler? |editor-last=Piper |editor-first=Ernst |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last=Fest |first=Joachim |author-link=Joachim Fest |chapter=Postscript, April 21, 1987 |pages=264–265 |title=Forever In The Shadow of Hitler? |editor-last=Piper |editor-first=Ernst |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref> [[Hagen Schulze]],<ref>{{cite book |last=Schulze |first=Hagen |author-link=Hagen Schulze |chapter=Questions We Have To Face: No Historical Stance without National Identity |pages=93–97 [94] |title=Forever In The Shadow of Hitler? |editor-last=Piper |editor-first=Ernst |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref> Horst Möller,<ref>{{cite book |last=Möller |first=Horst |chapter=What May Not Be, Cannot Be: A Plea for Rendering Factual the Controversy about Recent History |pages=216–221 [216–218] |title=Forever In The Shadow of Hitler? |editor-last=Piper |editor-first=Ernst |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref> [[Imanuel Geiss]]<ref>{{cite book |last=Geiss |first=Imanuel |author-link=Imanuel Geiss |chapter=On the ''Historikerstreit'' |pages=254–258 [256] |title=Forever In the Shadow Of Hitler? |editor-last=Piper |editor-first=Ernst |publisher=[[Humanities Press]] |location=Atlantic Highlands |date=1993}}</ref> and Klaus Hildebrand.<ref>Hildebrand, Klaus, "The Age of Tyrants: History and Politics The Administrators of the Enlightenment, the Risk of Scholarship and the Preservation of a Worldview A Reply to Jürgen Habermas", pp. 50–55, & "He Who Wants To Escape the Abyss Will Have Sound It Very Precisely: Is the New German History Writing Revisionist?" pp. 188–195 from ''Forever In The Shadow of Hitler?'' ed. Piper (1993).</ref> In turn, Habermas was supported by historians such as [[Martin Broszat]],<ref>Broszat, Martin, "Where the Roads Part: History Is Not A Suitable Substitute for a Religion of Nationalism", pp. 123–129, ''Forever In The Shadow of Hitler?'' ed. Piper (1993), p. 127.</ref> [[Eberhard Jäckel]],<ref>Jäckel, Eberhard, "The Impoverished Practice of Insinuation: The Singular Aspect of National Socialist Crimes Cannot Be Denied", pp. 74–78 from ''Forever In The Shadow of Hitler?'' ed. Piper (1993), pp. 74–75.</ref> [[Hans Mommsen]],<ref>Mommsen, Hans, "The New Historical Consciousness and the Relativizing of National Socialism", pp. 114–124 from ''Forever In The Shadow of Hitler?'' ed. Piper (1993), pp. 114–115.</ref> and [[Hans-Ulrich Wehler]].<ref>Evans, Richard, ''In Hitler's Shadow'', New York: Pantheon Books, 1989, pp. 159–160.</ref>

=== Habermas and Derrida ===
Habermas and [[Jacques Derrida]] engaged in a series of disputes beginning in the 1980s and culminating in a mutual understanding and friendship in the late 1990s that lasted until Derrida's death in 2004.<ref name="Derrida, J. Pp. 300-306">{{Cite book |last=Derrida |first=J |author-link=Jacques Derrida |chapter=Honesty of Thought |title=The Derrida-Habermas Reader |editor=Lasse Thomassen |place=Chicago |publisher=[[The University of Chicago Press]] |year=2006 |page=302 |isbn=978-0-226-79683-3}}</ref> They originally came in contact when Habermas invited Derrida to speak at the [[University of Frankfurt am Main]] in 1984. The next year Habermas published "Beyond a Temporalized Philosophy of Origins: Derrida" in ''[[The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity]]'' in which he described Derrida's method as being unable to provide a foundation for social critique.<ref>Thomassen, L. "Introduction: Between Deconstruction and Rational Reconstruction" in ''The Derrida-Habermas Reader'', ed. Thomassen (2006), pp. 1–7. P.2.</ref> Derrida, citing Habermas as an example, remarked that, "those who have accused me of reducing philosophy to literature or logic to rhetoric ... have visibly and carefully avoided reading me".<ref>Derrida, J., "Is There a Philosophical Language?" in ''The Derrida-Habermas Reader'', ed. Thomassen (2006), pp. 35–45. P.37.</ref> After Derrida's final rebuttal in 1989 the two philosophers did not continue, but, as Derrida described it, groups in the academy "conducted a kind of 'war', in which we ourselves never took part, either personally or directly".<ref name="Derrida, J. Pp. 300-306"/>

At the end of the 1990s, Habermas approached Derrida at a party held at an American university where both were lecturing. They then met in Paris over dinner, and participated afterwards in many joint projects. In 2000 they held a joint seminar on problems of philosophy, right, ethics, and politics at the University of Frankfurt.<ref name="Derrida, J. Pp. 300-306"/> In December 2000, in Paris, Habermas gave a lecture entitled "How to answer the ethical question?" at the ''Judeities. Questions for Jacques Derrida'' conference organized by Joseph Cohen and Raphael Zagury-Orly. The conference volume was published in English by Fordham University Press (2007).<ref>{{Cite book |last=Brumlik |first=Micha |editor-last=Brunkhorst |editor-first=Hauke |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Habermas_Handbook/NFg3DwAAQBAJ |title=The Habermas Handbook |editor2-last=Kreide |editor2-first=Regina |editor3-last=Lafont |editor3-first=Cristina |date=2017-10-24 |publisher=Columbia University Press |isbn=978-0-231-53588-5 |language=en |chapter=Jewish Philosophy}}</ref>

In the aftermath of [[September 11, 2001 attacks|the 11 September attacks]], Derrida and Habermas laid out their individual opinions on 9/11 and the [[war on terror]] in [[Giovanna Borradori]]'s ''Philosophy in a Time of Terror: Dialogues with Jürgen Habermas and Jacques Derrida''. In early 2003, both Habermas and Derrida were very active in [[Opposition to the Iraq War|opposing]] the coming [[Iraq War]]; in a manifesto that later became the book ''[[Old Europe, New Europe, Core Europe]]'', the two called for a tighter unification of the states of the European Union in order to create a power capable of opposing [[American foreign policy]]. Derrida wrote a foreword expressing his unqualified subscription to Habermas's declaration of February 2003 ("February 15, or, What Binds Europeans Together: Plea for a Common Foreign Policy, Beginning in Core Europe") in the book, which was a reaction to the [[George W. Bush administration|Bush administration]]'s demands upon European nations for support in the coming Iraq War.<ref>Habermas, J. and Derrida, J. "February 15, Or What Binds Europeans Together: A Plea for a Common Foreign Policy, beginning in the Core of Europe" in ''The Derrida-Habermas Reader'', ed. Thomassen (2006), pp. 270–277. P. 302.</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=McAleer |first=Graham |date=2026-05-01 |title=The Last Rationalist |url=https://lawliberty.org/the-last-rationalist/ |access-date=2026-05-08 |website=Law & Liberty |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== Religious dialogue ===
Habermas's attitudes toward religion changed throughout the years. Analyst Phillippe Portier identifies three phases in Habermas's attitude towards this social sphere: the first, in the decade of 1980, when the younger Jürgen, in the spirit of Marx, argued against religion seeing it as an "alienating reality" and "control tool"; the second phase, from the mid-1980s to the beginning of the 21st century, when he stopped discussing it and, as a secular commentator, relegated it to matters of private life; and the third, from then on, when Habermas saw a positive social role of religion.<ref>{{cite web | url = http://www.elmundo.es/elmundo/2011/10/07/cultura/1317980044.html
| title = 'San' Jürgen Habermas| last = Sánchez |first = Rosalía|date = 7 October 2011| website =[[El Mundo (Spain)|El Mundo]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150110111154/http://www.elmundo.es/elmundo/2011/10/07/cultura/1317980044.html |archive-date=10 January 2015 |access-date=10 January 2015}}</ref>

In an interview in 1999 Habermas had stated:

{{blockquote|For the normative self-understanding of modernity, Christianity has functioned as more than just a precursor or catalyst. Universalistic [[egalitarianism]], from which sprang the ideals of freedom and a collective life in solidarity, the autonomous conduct of life and emancipation, the individual morality of conscience, human rights and democracy, is the direct legacy of the Judaic ethic of justice and the Christian ethic of love. This legacy, substantially unchanged, has been the object of a continual critical reappropriation and reinterpretation. Up to this very day there is no alternative to it. And in light of the current challenges of a post-national constellation, we must draw sustenance now, as in the past, from this substance. Everything else is idle postmodern talk.<ref>Habermas, Jurgen, ''Religion and Rationality: Essays on Reason, God, and Modernity'', ed. Eduardo Mendieta, MIT Press, 2002, p. 149. And Habermas, Jurgen, ''Time of Transitions'', Polity Press, 2006, pp. 150–151.</ref><ref>[http://www.firstprinciplesjournal.com/articles.aspx?article=1342&theme=home&page=4&loc=b&type=ctbf ''First Principles Journal– Recovering the Western Soul'', Wilfred M. McClay] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130530224950/http://www.firstprinciplesjournal.com/articles.aspx?article=1342&theme=home&page=4&loc=b&type=ctbf |date=30 May 2013 }} (from IR 42:1, Spring 2007) – 01/01/09. Accessed: 2 December 2012.</ref><ref>[https://books.google.com/books?id=YRthfWwOoJMC&pg=PA48 ''Secularization and Cultural Criticism: Religion, Nation, and Modernity'', Vincent P. Pecora.] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211221212825/https://books.google.com/books?id=YRthfWwOoJMC&pg=PA48 |date=21 December 2021 }}.</ref>}}

The original German (from the Habermas Forum website) of the disputed quotation is:

{{blockquote|''Das Christentum ist für das normative Selbstverständnis der Moderne nicht nur eine Vorläufergestalt oder ein Katalysator gewesen. Der egalitäre Universalismus, aus dem die Ideen von Freiheit und solidarischem Zusammenleben, von autonomer Lebensführung und Emanzipation, von individueller Gewissensmoral, Menschenrechten und Demokratie entsprungen sind, ist unmittelbar ein Erbe der jüdischen Gerechtigkeits- und der christlichen Liebesethik. In der Substanz unverändert, ist dieses Erbe immer wieder kritisch angeeignet und neu interpretiert worden. Dazu gibt es bis heute keine Alternative. Auch angesichts der aktuellen Herausforderungen einer postnationalen Konstellation zehren wir nach wie vor von dieser Substanz. Alles andere ist postmodernes Gerede.''|author=Jürgen Habermas|source=''Zeit der Übergänge'' (2001), p.&nbsp;174f.}}

This statement has been misquoted in a number of articles and books, where Habermas instead is quoted for saying:

{{blockquote|Christianity, and nothing else, is the ultimate foundation of liberty, conscience, human rights, and democracy, the benchmarks of Western civilization. To this day, we have no other options. We continue to nourish ourselves from this source. Everything else is postmodern chatter.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://habermas-rawls.blogspot.fi/2009/06/misquote-about-habermas-and.html|title=A misquote about Habermas and Christianity|date=2009-06-08|website=habermas-rawls.blogspot.fi|access-date=28 October 2017|archive-date=29 October 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171029120926/http://habermas-rawls.blogspot.fi/2009/06/misquote-about-habermas-and.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>Ambrose Ih-Ren Mong. ''Dialogue Derailed: Joseph Ratzinger's War against Pluralist Theology''. Wipf and Stock Publishers. p. 279</ref>}}

In his book ''Zwischen Naturalismus und Religion'' (Between Naturalism and Religion, 2005), Habermas stated that the forces of religious strength, as a result of multiculturalism and immigration, are stronger than in previous decades, and, therefore, there is a need of tolerance which must be understood as a two-way street: secular people need to [[tolerate]] the role of religious people in the public square and vice versa.<ref>[http://www.resetdoc.org/EN/Habermas-Istanbul.php A "post-secular" society – what does that mean?] {{Webarchive |url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090329090452/http://www.resetdoc.org/EN/Habermas-Istanbul.php |date= 29 March 2009 }} by Jurgen Habermas, June 2008.</ref><ref>{{cite web |first=Javier |last=Espinosa |url= http://www.uclm.es/actividades0304/seminarios/spinoza/pdf/cuadernos/22.pdf |title=The religion in the public sphere. Habermas, Toland and Spinoza |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160108181837/http://www.uclm.es/actividades0304/seminarios/spinoza/pdf/cuadernos/22.pdf |archive-date=8 January 2016 |publisher=Universidad de Castilla-La Mancha}}</ref>

{{anchor|XVI}}In early 2007, [[Ignatius Press]] published a dialogue between Habermas and the then [[Prefect of the Congregation for the Doctrine of the Faith]] of the [[Holy Office]] Joseph Ratzinger (elected as [[Pope Benedict XVI]] in 2005), entitled ''The Dialectics of Secularization''. The dialogue took place on 14 January 2004 after an invitation to both thinkers by the Catholic Academy of Bavaria in Munich.<ref>{{cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ERzoAPsS9usC&q=the+Dialectics+of+Secularization&pg=PP1|title=Dialectics of Secularization: On Reason and Religion|first1=Benedikt XVI|last1=(Papst)|first2=Jürgen|last2=Habermas|date=28 October 2017|publisher=Ignatius Press|access-date=28 October 2017|via=Google Books|isbn=978-1-58617-166-7}}</ref> It addressed contemporary questions such as:<ref>{{Cite book |last=Bartholomew |first=Craig G. |url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Introducing_Biblical_Hermeneutics/bpfQCgAAQBAJ |title=Introducing Biblical Hermeneutics: A Comprehensive Framework for Hearing God in Scripture |date=11 November 2015 |publisher=Baker Academic |isbn=978-1-4412-2775-1 |language=en |chapter=Philosophy and hermeneutics}}</ref>

* Is a public culture of [[reason]] and ordered [[liberty]] possible in our [[Post-metaphysical philosophy|post-metaphysical]] age?
* Is philosophy permanently cut adrift from its grounding in [[being]] and [[anthropology]]?
* Does this decline of rationality signal an opportunity or a deep crisis for religion itself?

In this debate a shift of Habermas became evident{{mdash}}in particular, his rethinking of the public role of religion.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Dreyer |first1=Jaco S. |last2=Pieterse |first2=Hennie J. C. |date=19 February 2010 |title=Religion in the public sphere: What can public theology learn from Habermas's latest work? |url=https://doi.org/10.4102/hts.v66i1.798 |journal=HTS Teologiese Studies / Theological Studies |volume=66 |issue=1 |doi=10.4102/hts.v66i1.798 |issn=2072-8050}}</ref> Habermas stated that he wrote as a "methodological atheist", which means that when doing philosophy or social science, he presumed nothing about particular religious beliefs.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Chambers |first=Simone |date=2007 |title=How Religion Speaks to the Agnostic: Habermas on the Persistent Value of Religion |url=https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-8675.2007.00434.x |journal=Constellations |volume=14 |issue=2 |page=212 |doi=10.1111/j.1467-8675.2007.00434.x |issn=1351-0487}}</ref> Yet while writing from this perspective his evolving position towards the role of religion in society led him to some challenging questions, and as a result conceding some ground in his dialogue with the future Pope, that would seem to have consequences which further complicated the positions he held about a communicative rational solution to the problems of modernity.{{Sfn|Dreyer|Pieterse|2010}}

In addition, Habermas has popularized the concept of "[[Postsecularism|post-secular]]" society, to refer to current times in which the idea of modernity is perceived as unsuccessful and at times, morally failed, so that, rather than a stratification or separation, a new peaceful dialogue and coexistence between faith and reason must be sought to learn mutually.<ref>{{cite journal|url=https://diarium.usal.es/fjherrerohe/files/2016/06/%C2%ABEl-Estado-debe-proteger-a-la-religi%C3%B3n%C2%BB.pdf|last=del Busto|first=Fernando|title=El Estado debe proteger a la religión|journal=El Comercio|page=6|date=31 August 2014|access-date=19 March 2026}}.</ref>

=== Socialist dialogue ===
Habermas sided with other 20th-century commentators on [[Karl Marx]] such as [[Hannah Arendt]] who indicated concerns with the limits of totalitarian perspectives often associated with Marx's overestimation of the emancipatory potential of the forces of production. Arendt had presented this in ''[[The Origins of Totalitarianism]]'', and Habermas extended this critique when writing about functional reductionism in the life-world (''[[Lifeworld and System: A Critique of Functionalist Reason]]'').<ref name="Habermas, Jurgen 1981"/> As Habermas states:

{{blockquote|... traditional Marxist analysis&nbsp;... today, when we use the means of the critique of political economy&nbsp;... can no longer make clear predictions: for that, one would still have to assume the autonomy of a self-reproducing economic system. I do not believe in such an autonomy. Precisely for this reason, the laws governing the economic system are no longer identical to the ones Marx analyzed. Of course, this does not mean that it would be wrong to analyze the mechanism which drives the economic system; but in order for the orthodox version of such an analysis to be valid, the influence of the political system would have to be ignored.<ref name="Habermas, Jurgen 1981"/>}}

In 1973, Habermas noted "the incompatibility of the imperatives that rule the capitalistic economic system with a democratic process for forming the public will". His critique of capitalism focused on its [[technocratic]] tendencies.{{sfn|Ahmad|2025|p=1347}} Habermas reiterated the positions that what refuted Marx and his theory of [[class struggle]] was the "pacification of class conflict" by the [[welfare state]], which had developed in the West "since 1945" thanks to "a reformist relying on the instruments of [[Keynesian economics]]".<ref>{{Cite book|last=Habermas|first=Jürgen|title=Theory of Communicative Action|publisher=[[Beacon Press]]|year=1987|volume=2|pages=348|translator-last=Thomas|translator-first=McCarthy}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite book|last=Losurdo|first=Domenico|title=Class Struggle|publisher=[[Palgrave Macmillan]]|year=2016|isbn=978-1-137-52387-7|pages=3|doi=10.1057/978-1-349-70660-0|s2cid=265035687 |lccn=2016940579}}</ref> Italian philosopher and historian [[Domenico Losurdo]] criticized the main point of these claims as "marked by the absence of a question that should be obvious: was the advent of the welfare state the inevitable result of a tendency inherent in [[capitalism]]? Or was it the result of political and social mobilization by the subaltern classes—in the final analysis, of a class struggle? Had the German philosopher posed this question, perhaps he would have avoided assuming the permanence of the welfare state, whose precariousness and progressive dismantlement are now obvious to everyone."<ref name=":0"/>

In the 1990s, Habermas criticized [[Third Way]]-style liberals and social democrats that dominated the centre-left, arguing that economically they were "barely distinguishable" from the political right.<ref name="Scheuerman 2017">{{cite magazine |last=Scheuerman |first=William E. |date=12 April 2017 |title=Habermas and the Fate of Democracy |url=https://www.bostonreview.net/articles/william-e-scheuerman-habermas-and-fate-democracy/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250708233217mp_/https://www.bostonreview.net/articles/william-e-scheuerman-habermas-and-fate-democracy |archive-date=8 July 2025 |access-date=17 March 2026 |magazine=Boston Review}}</ref> His ideas had some influence on the [[Chinese New Left]] at the turn of the millennium.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Hui |first1=Wang |title=The End of the Revolution: China and the Limits of Modernity |date=2009 |publisher=Verso |page=72}}</ref> In November 2016, Habermas reiterated his call for left-leaning political parties in Europe to join arms and "go on the offensive against social inequality by embarking upon a coordinated and cross-border taming of unregulated markets".<ref name="Scheuerman 2017"/> In ''The Philosopher: Habermas and Us'', Philipp Felsch reminded readers that "Habermas never ceased identifying as a socialist. But his was a socialism that by the 2000s was unapologetically reformist, even if willing to learn from the radical left."<ref>{{cite magazine |last=McManus |first=Matt |date=15 March 2026 |title=Jürgen Habermas Showed What Philosophy Could Be |url=https://jacobin.com/2026/03/habermas-obituary-critical-theory-philosophy |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260315174817/https://jacobin.com/2026/03/habermas-obituary-critical-theory-philosophy |archive-date=15 March 2026 |access-date=17 March 2026 |magazine=Jacobin}}</ref>

=== European Union ===
Referencing Hegel's concept of the ''[[cunning of reason]]'', used to describe the progressive realisation of freedom in history unbeknown to individuals, Habermas has stated that the [[euro]] represents the "cunning of economic reason".{{sfn|Ahmad|2025|p=1347}} During the [[European debt crisis]], Habermas criticized [[Angela Merkel]]'s leadership in Europe. In 2013, Habermas clashed with [[Wolfgang Streeck]], who argued the kind of European federalism espoused by Habermas was the root of the continent's crisis.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Oltermann |first=Philip |date=16 July 2015 |title=Merkel 'gambling away' Germany's reputation over Greece, says Habermas |url=https://www.theguardian.com/business/2015/jul/16/merkel-gambling-away-germanys-reputation-over-greece-says-habermas |access-date=17 March 2026 |work=The Guardian |issn=0261-3077}}</ref> Habermas declared himself a supporter of [[Emmanuel Macron]] ahead of the [[2017 French presidential election]].{{sfn|Neumann|2019|p=11}}

